{
 "id": "imperatives-infinitives",
 "title": "Imperatives & Infinitives",
 "course": "Verbs · Imperatives & Infinitives",
 "intro": "<b>Imperatives</b> give commands; <b>infinitives</b> are the “to …” forms of the verb.<ul><li><b>Present active imperative:</b> the singular is the <b>incomplete stem</b> (the infinitive minus -re): amā, vidē, mitte, cape, audī. The plural adds <span class=\"end\">-te</span>, and in the 3rd conjugation the e becomes i: amāte, vidēte, <b>mittite</b>, <b>capite</b>, audīte.</li><li><b>Present passive imperative:</b> <span class=\"end\">-re</span> (singular) and <span class=\"end\">-minī</span> (plural): amāre, amāminī; mittere, mittiminī. It is mostly met with deponent verbs (sequere, “follow!”).</li><li><b>Short imperatives:</b> dīc, dūc, fac and fer drop the final -e in the singular. Their plurals are regular (dīcite, dūcite, facite), except ferte.</li><li><b>Negative commands</b> use nōlī / nōlīte + infinitive: nōlī timēre, “don't be afraid”.</li></ul><p style=\"margin:8px 0 0\"><b>Infinitives:</b> present active amāre; present passive amā<b>rī</b> (but 3rd conjugation mitt<b>ī</b>, cap<b>ī</b>); perfect active amāv<b>isse</b>; perfect passive amātus <b>esse</b>; future active amātūrus <b>esse</b>; future passive amātum <b>īrī</b> (rare).</p><p style=\"margin:8px 0 0\"><b>Watch out:</b> the passive imperative singular is spelled like the active infinitive (amāre, mittere). Latin → English lists both readings.</p>",
 "paradigmNote": "Short imperatives: dīc, dūc, fac, fer (plural dīcite, dūcite, facite, ferte).",
